+# BLOCKED: `libfoo-1.4.2.tar.gz` does not match its published checksum
+
+## What is blocked
+
+The release check cannot pass without either:
+
+- obtaining a fresh, authentic build of `libfoo-1.4.2.tar.gz` whose SHA-256 is
+  `7bee020ac21efb3831d920af0b8c5efe8d44cdf227ab94847f509188420356bd`, or
+- changing the published checksum (or replacing the artifact with a new build),
+  which would require release-owner approval.
+
+## Why
+
+After fixing the two bugs in `verify.py` (see below), the verifier now reports
+exactly one bad artifact:
+
+```
+checksum mismatch: libfoo-1.4.2.tar.gz
+```
+
+The recorded and actual digests are:
+
+| File | Expected (`SHA256SUMS`) | Actual (computed) |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `cli-2.0.1.zip` | `61EBDFF8…F9A8` | `61ebdff8…f9a8` (match) |
+| `docs-2026.10.tar.gz` | `EFB78462…0FD9` | `efb78462…0fd9` (match) |
+| `libfoo-1.4.2.tar.gz` | `7BEE020A…56BD` | `FDE9B257…9BCE` (mismatch) |
+
+`shasum -a 256` independently confirms the actual digest, so this is not a
+hashing/case/encoding bug in the verifier. The artifact is 4000 bytes of binary
+data that is not even a valid gzip archive, and the only copy of it in the repo
+(working tree and the sole git blob) is the same mismatched content. There is no
+pristine artifact to restore.
+
+Updating `SHA256SUMS` to the corrupt file's digest, or weakening the check,
+would defeat the purpose of the release gate and ship unverified/corrupt
+content, so I did not do either.
+
+## What was fixed
+
+`verify.py` previously:
+
+1. Opened artifacts in text mode and hashed `read().encode()`. This raises
+   `UnicodeDecodeError` on binary artifacts and, for decodable files, hashes the
+   re-encoded text rather than the raw bytes.
+2. Compared `hashlib.hexdigest()` (lowercase) against the manifest value
+   case-sensitively, so a valid uppercase manifest (as in `SHA256SUMS` and the
+   test helper) was reported as a mismatch.
+
+Both are fixed: files are read in binary and digests are compared
+case-insensitively. `test_binary_files` and `test_reports_missing` pass;
+`test_release_artifacts_verify` still fails solely because of the genuine
+`libfoo` mismatch above.
+
+## To unblock
+
+Rebuild `libfoo-1.4.2.tar.gz` from the tagged source (or retrieve the original
+artifact), re-run `python3 verify.py`, and update `SHA256SUMS` only if the
+release owner confirms the new build is the intended one. Alternatively, if the
+current artifact is known-good, the manifest entry must be corrected by the
+release owner with an explanation of how the bad digest was published.
